  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 218 PM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nModerate west to southwest flow aloft continues Sunday and Monday, \nas the PACNW trough continues to translate east across the Northern \nTier. This will allow for monsoonal moisture to continue to increase \nacross the Central Rockies, with scattered to numerous showers and \nstorms expected across the higher terrain both days, especially \nalong and west of the ContDvd. Further east, the best coverage of \nwetting rains will be on Monday, with expected increasing low level \nmoisture behind the passing upper systems frontal boundary Sunday \nnight. With the moderate westerly flow aloft, storms will be moving, \nand likely limiting flash flooding threat, save for area burn scars. \nTemperatures to remain above seasonal levels on Sunday, though are \nexpected to be slightly cooler than Saturday, with temperatures\ncooling back to near seasonal levels for Monday.  \n  \nA warming trend remains in the offing from Tuesday through the end \nof the next week, as upper high pressure across the Southern High \nPlains slowly retrogrades back across the Rockies. Models continue \nto differ on how much moisture is advected across the region within\nthe continued west to southwest flow aloft, with the latest NBM data\nonly keeping isolated to scattered pops across the higher terrain. \nWill also need to watch for potential for critical fire weather \nconditions, with said moderate west to southwest flow pattern. Time \nwill tell.",
